Response: 
Bachelor of Computer Science and Master of Data Science Product Brief

University Partner Review

This brief brings together the proposed course proposition, priority audiences and competitive position for the four-year online double degree. The opportunity is to offer an accessible pathway to two qualifications while differentiating through emerging technology, embedded industry involvement and responsible innovation. Partner feedback is requested on the claims, evidence and delivery details required before the proposition moves into market.

Product summary

Product element

Proposed position

Award

Bachelor of Computer Science and Master of Data Science

Duration

Four years, with three years focused on computer science and the fourth on data science

Delivery

Online study designed for students who cannot attend regular face-to-face classes

Entry

ATAR 75; no advanced mathematics prerequisite, with assumed knowledge only

Related pathway

Three-year standalone Bachelor of Computer Science

Core focus

Computer science fundamentals, artificial intelligence, digital twins, quantum computing, cybersecurity and digital trust

Industry involvement

Curriculum input and learning content from AWS, Cisco, Microsoft and Palo Alto, with potential internships, scholarships and graduate pathways

Student support

Webinars, one-to-one consultations and AI-enabled support available at any time

Recommended market position

Position the course as an accessible, industry-connected online pathway for students who want both broad computer science capability and advanced data science skills. The four-year structure gives students a master's qualification sooner than a conventional sequential pathway, while the online format removes the location constraint present in the closest Australian alternatives.

Proposed value proposition

Build practical capability across computer science and data science, learn with leading technology partners, and graduate with two qualifications in four years through flexible online study.

Primary reasons to choose

Two qualifications in four years, creating a faster route to master's-level capability.

An online format and ATAR 75 entry point that broaden access without removing academic expectations.

Coverage of emerging technologies beyond AI, with responsible innovation embedded across the curriculum.

Sustained involvement from major technology companies rather than isolated guest presentations.

Priority audiences and messaging

The course should lead with one core proposition, then adjust the emphasis for two priority audiences. Both groups value employability and an efficient pathway, but they assess risk differently: school leavers look for confidence, relevance and support, while working adults need flexibility and evidence that the course will improve their career options.

Audience

Ambitious school leaver or early career starter

Career changer or technology upskiller

Profile

Usually 17 to 24; recent Year 12 graduate or early-career learner; may live outside easy reach of a campus

Usually 25 to 40; employed and balancing study with work or caring responsibilities

What matters

A credible degree, clear career options, current technology content, accessible entry and confidence that online students receive strong support

Flexible study, practical programming and data capability, recognised qualifications and a manageable route into or forward within technology

Motivation

Earn a bachelor and master's in four years; develop skills across computer science and data science; improve employability

Change career, progress into higher-value roles, increase earning potential and gain both foundational and advanced capability efficiently

Likely concerns

Whether online study will feel engaging; confidence with mathematics; cost; graduate outcomes; ability to stay motivated

Time commitment; returning to study; whether the curriculum is practical; recognition of prior experience; return on investment

Message emphasis

Future-facing skills, two qualifications, industry exposure, structured support and accessible entry

Online flexibility, practical application, career progression, industry relevance and a clear four-year pathway

Messaging framework

Message pillar

Student value

Evidence required

Two qualifications in four years

A more efficient route from undergraduate study to advanced data science capability

Confirmed course map, award structure, duration and progression requirements

Industry embedded learning

Learning reflects the platforms, practices and capability employers use

Partner roles in curriculum and delivery, certification alignment, internship arrangements and examples of industry-led activity

Technology with responsibility

Students learn to build useful technology while considering privacy, accountability, accessibility and security

Named units, assessments, research examples and the responsible innovation framework

Online study with active support

Students can study around other commitments without being left to manage the course alone

Teaching model, contact hours, advisor access, webinars, consultation model and scope of AI-enabled support

Tone and content direction

Marketing should be practical and specific. Show what students will build, which technologies they will use, how industry partners contribute and where the combined qualification can lead. Use student-friendly explanations of mathematics expectations and online support. Representation should actively include women and students from varied backgrounds without framing them as exceptions in technology.

Competitive position and partner decisions

The earlier competitor review found only two close Australian integrated bachelor and master comparators. Both require campus attendance. This creates a credible opening for an online course that combines computer science and data science within four years, provided the University can substantiate the proposed delivery, industry and career claims.

